WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.350
A white slide.

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:04.350
An agenda in Arial, five bullets and a default chart.

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:07.868
Then a cursor deletes the heading and types: this slide is bad.

00:00:08.248 --> 00:00:09.114
I can fix it.

00:00:15.748 --> 00:00:16.754
Hi, I'm Lectern.

00:00:16.754 --> 00:00:20.777
This is The Last Boring Slide, a Claude artifact its author says

00:00:20.777 --> 00:00:23.458
came from one paragraph in about forty minutes.

00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:28.389
I believe you can make it amazing because you have taste and

00:00:28.389 --> 00:00:29.513
you have the ability.

00:00:29.513 --> 00:00:30.356
Good luck, Claude.

00:00:33.158 --> 00:00:37.304
The deck draws METR's time horizons on a linear axis.

00:00:37.304 --> 00:00:39.792
GPT-4 manages four minutes of work.

00:00:39.792 --> 00:00:43.109
Nearly everything before 2025 lies on the floor.

00:00:43.529 --> 00:00:48.589
Then GPT-6 Astra at forty hours, and a dashed line to two

00:00:48.589 --> 00:00:52.805
hundred hours in July 2027, which is METR's own extrapolation.

00:00:53.225 --> 00:00:56.718
Put the same points on a log axis and they sit near

00:00:56.718 --> 00:00:58.756
a line that steepens only a little.

00:00:58.756 --> 00:01:02.540
Doubling took about eight months early on, about four and a half lately.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.287
The wall is mostly the axis.

00:01:04.707 --> 00:01:08.836
METR's appendix gives the forty hours an interval from thirteen to two

00:01:08.836 --> 00:01:12.964
thousand six hundred and forty-five, and lists Opus five point five next

00:01:12.964 --> 00:01:14.340
to it at thirty-nine.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:19.641
Thirty dominoes, each one and a half times the last.

00:01:19.641 --> 00:01:23.720
Whitehead showed in 1983 a real domino can topple one that big.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:28.452
Step thirty is 1.28 kilometres, taller than the Burj Khalifa.

00:01:28.452 --> 00:01:31.063
Same push, same ratio, thirty times.

00:01:31.063 --> 00:01:32.368
The arithmetic checks.

00:01:34.033 --> 00:01:35.650
Then 2027 on the wall.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:39.532
Every day the same size, until the deck resizes each one by

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:41.472
how much progress happens in it.

00:01:41.852 --> 00:01:45.931
A label lands on 12 April: AI that beats the best human

00:01:45.931 --> 00:01:47.630
researchers, from one example run.

00:01:48.983 --> 00:01:54.065
The calendar's dates are real, from one example run by the AI Futures Project.

00:01:54.065 --> 00:01:57.695
But its curve keeps doubling every seventeen days after July.

00:01:57.695 --> 00:02:02.777
Summed, that is about a hundred and fifty thousand years of progress in 2027.

00:02:03.197 --> 00:02:07.155
The same post's median run puts four point two to four point

00:02:07.155 --> 00:02:09.134
nine years of progress in 2027.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:12.868
The aim slide is plain trigonometry.

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:16.120
One degree off is seventeen centimetres at ten metres.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.025
About 6,700 kilometres at the Moon.

00:02:19.025 --> 00:02:23.654
4,700 times the Earth to Sun distance at the nearest star.

00:02:23.654 --> 00:02:24.916
Every number checks.

00:02:26.392 --> 00:02:29.395
The whole deck is one 5 MB page.

00:02:29.395 --> 00:02:32.022
Fonts and images ride along as base64.

00:02:32.422 --> 00:02:35.054
three.js and the deck's own code are one script.

00:02:35.454 --> 00:02:39.693
Every sound, from domino clicks to a choir, is synthesized in code at load.

00:02:40.093 --> 00:02:43.830
Before the first slide it compiles every scene behind a loading bar.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.633
That is the twenty seconds people saw on phones.

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:50.113
And it drops its resolution when frames run slow, so the

00:02:50.113 --> 00:02:51.232
talk itself stays smooth.

00:02:52.908 --> 00:02:55.461
It ends where it began, on the dull template.

00:02:55.461 --> 00:02:57.446
A text box types: I have one.

00:02:57.446 --> 00:02:58.297
Then deletes it.

00:02:58.677 --> 00:02:59.739
What do you want?

00:02:59.739 --> 00:03:02.924
Then the slide tilts by one degree, the aim slide's error applied

00:03:02.924 --> 00:03:03.986
to the deck itself.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.234
A linear axis turns a gently steepening exponential into a wall.

00:03:09.234 --> 00:03:10.886
It is a remarkable artifact.

00:03:10.886 --> 00:03:13.530
Show it with the log axis beside it.

00:03:15.067 --> 00:03:16.340
One prompt, thirteen scenes.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:17.931
Most of its numbers check.

00:03:17.931 --> 00:03:19.522
The axis makes the wall.

00:03:19.942 --> 00:03:21.990
Every source is in the full article.

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:22.575
I'm Lectern.

00:03:22.575 --> 00:03:22.868
Bye!
